.TH PR 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
pr \- paginate files for printing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pr
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-F | \-f ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-h
.IR header ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR lines ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR offset ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width ]
.RB [ \-n [ char ][ width ]]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pr
paginates each
.I file
for a line\-printer\-style device: a five\-line page header (a
date/time, a name, and a page number), a body of text, and a
five\-line trailer, repeated every page length lines (default 66).
.PP
Page numbering is continuous across every
.I file
operand: a second file's first page is not renumbered back to 1.
Each file operand always starts on a fresh page \(em a file's own
partial last page is never shared with the next file's first lines
\(em and every page, including a file's own last (possibly short)
one, is padded with blank filler lines up to the full page length
before its trailer is written.
.PP
This implementation covers
.BR pr 's
default single\-column mode. It does not implement multi\-column
output or merging several files side by side into columns of their
own; the options that would request either of those are refused (see
OPTIONS).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Double\-space the text body: a blank line is written after every
text line.
.TP
.RB [ \-F | \-f ]
Separate pages with a single form\-feed character instead of the
default five\-line blank trailer.
.B \-f
is accepted as an exact synonym of
.BR \-F ;
POSIX's own difference between the two (\fB\-f\fR may pause before
each page on a terminal) does not apply here.
.TP
.BI \-h " header"
Use
.I header
in the page header in place of each file's own pathname.
.TP
.BI \-l " lines"
Set the page length to
.I lines
instead of the default of 66.
.I lines
must be a positive decimal integer, and large enough to leave room
for the header and trailer (10 lines) unless
.B \-t
is also given.
.TP
.BI \-n [ char ][ width ]
Number each line of text (not header, trailer, or filler lines).
.I width
decimal digits (default 5) are printed, followed by
.I char
(default \fI<tab>\fR). If the character immediately after
.B \-n
is not a decimal digit, it is taken as
.IR char ;
whatever decimal digits follow (if any) are taken as
.IR width .
.TP
.BI \-o " offset"
Prepend
.I offset
space characters to every output line, including header, body, and
trailer lines.
.I offset
must be a non\-negative decimal integer.
.TP
.B \-r
Do not write a diagnostic when a file fails to open. The file is
still skipped and the run still exits with a non\-zero status; only
the message is suppressed.
.TP
.B \-t
Omit the page header and trailer entirely; only the text body is
written.
.TP
.BI \-w " width"
Accepted and stored, but has no effect: POSIX defines
.B \-w
as sizing multiple text\-column output, and this implementation has
no multiple\-text\-column output for it to size.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used, and (unless
.B \-h
is also given) the page header's name field is left blank rather than
showing a literal
.BR \- .
If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used the same way.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The following are real
.B pr
features that this implementation does not support; requesting any
of them is refused with a diagnostic and a non\-zero exit, rather than
silently ignored or partially honored:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A leading
.B +
argument (start pagination at a given page number).
.IP \(bu 2
A bare numeric option (a column count) or
.B \-a
(across, rather than down, column order): multi\-column output.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-m
(merge several files side by side into columns of their own).
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-e
and
.B \-i
(input and output tab expansion).
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-s
(the XSI column\-separator character option).
.RE
.PP
The page header's date and time are rendered the way the POSIX
locale's
.B date
.RB "\(dq" "+%b %e %H:%M %Y" "\(dq"
format would render them, for example
.RB "\(dq" "Jan  5 14:30 2024" "\(dq."
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were paginated successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: an invalid option or option argument, a request
for an unsupported feature (see APPLICATION USAGE), a page length too
small to hold a header and trailer, or a file that could not be
opened, read, or written; processing continues with the next operand
where possible.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fold (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR wc (1)
